<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philippe Leclerc de Hauteclocque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1902">1902</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philippe Leclerc de Hauteclocque was a French general who joined the Free French in 1940. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> he led raids into Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufra">Kufra</a> in 1941 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> in 1942–1943, then commanded the 2nd Armoured Division, which liberated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in August 1944 and Strasbourg in November. He died in an air crash in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 1947 and was made Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> posthumously.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philippe-leclerc-de-hauteclocque.jpg" alt="Philippe Leclerc de Hauteclocque" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Downey John, from the US Office of War Information · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leclerc-casque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philippe de Hauteclocque was born on 22 November 1902 at Belloy-Saint-Léonard in the Somme, the fifth of six children of Adrien de Hauteclocque, from an old line of country nobility whose ancestors had served in the Fifth Crusade and in Saint Louis's Eighth Crusade of 1270. He graduated from the military academy of Saint-Cyr on 1 October 1924 and, having chosen the cavalry, graduated first in his class from the Cavalry School at Saumur in 1925. He married Thérèse de Gargan on 10 August 1925, and they had six children. In 1933 he was awarded the croix de guerre for leading goumiers in an attack at Bou Amdoun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-france">Battle of France</a> in 1940, was captured, escaped, was wounded on 15 June and taken prisoner again, and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 25 July 1940. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> promoted him to major. Sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a> as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, he adopted the name Leclerc so that his wife and children would not be put at risk. He then led a force against Gabon, whose local leader supported Vichy France, in a campaign from 12 October to 12 November 1940. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> he led raids into Italian Libya, and after his forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufra">Kufra</a> he had his men swear an oath to fight on until their flag flew over Strasbourg Cathedral.</p><p>His force, known as L Force, became the 2e Division Blindée in 1943, often called the Division Leclerc. It fought in Normandy and took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-of-paris">liberation of Paris</a> and Strasbourg. After the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> he commanded the French Far East Expeditionary Corps, represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the Japanese surrender in Tokyo Bay on 2 September 1945, and arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 5 October 1945. He saw quickly that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> needed a political solution and was recalled to France in 1946.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leclerc was appointed Inspector of Land Forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. On 28 November 1947 his B-25 Mitchell crashed near Colomb-Béchar in French Algeria, killing everyone aboard. His funeral was held at Notre Dame de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and he was interred at Les Invalides. He was made Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> posthumously on 23 August 1952, the anniversary of the day his division entered Paris. The Leclerc tank is named after him, and two streets in Paris bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philippe Leclerc de Hauteclocque” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In August 1940 Governor Félix Éboué declared for Free France, and <b>Leclerc</b>'s forces struck north from Chad into Libya.</blockquote><a href="/state/chad-french-colony">Chad (French colony)</a></li><li><blockquote>In late 1942 and January 1943 Free French forces under <b>Philippe Leclerc</b> advanced from Chad (French colony) and took the Fezzan from Italian Libya.</blockquote><a href="/state/fezzan-ghadames-military-territory">Fezzan-Ghadames Military Territory</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Belloy-Saint-Léonard, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Béchar, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.91,1.9&amp;year=1931" aria-label="Belloy-Saint-Léonard on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4594 427 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Philippe Leclerc de Hauteclocque"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5044 697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5044 697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.91,1.9&amp;year=1931">What was here in 1931? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
